{
  "_id": "6682bd81ba0e94a3c4025d11",
  "type": "numerical",
  "level": 2,
  "questionLabels": [],
  "question": {
    "text": "A square loop of side <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math> and resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>7</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></math> is placed vertically in the east-west plane. A uniform magnetic field of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>20</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></math> is set up across the plane in the north-east direction. The magnetic field is decreased to zero in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math> at a steady rate. Then, the magnitude of induced emf is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mi>x</mi></msqrt><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>. The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math> is _______.",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "2",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/6cc14ab6-3a8c-44cb-84af-b3d461f7efac.png\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 266px;\"/></p><p>The area vector for the square loop is given by</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>→</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo> </mo><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo> </mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p><p>According to the above diagram, the magnetic field is given by</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mo>→</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></math></p><p>Hence, the magnitude of induced emf can be calculated as follows</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtable columnalign=\"right center left\" columnspacing=\"0px\"><mtr><mtd><mi>e</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mfrac><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mfrac><mrow><mover><mi>B</mi><mo>→</mo></mover><mo>·</mo><mover><mi>A</mi><mo>→</mo></mover><mo>-</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mn>1</mn></mfrac></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mfrac><mrow><mfenced><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mfenced><mo>·</mo><mfenced><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo> </mo><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo> </mo></mrow></mfenced><mo>-</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mn>1</mn></mfrac><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></p><p>Hence, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math>.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
